Some X96Max_P boards wire a fourth USB2 port that the controller does not
advertise. hcs_params1 reports MaxPorts = 3, yet PORTSC4 shows a connected
device (CCS = 1, PP = 1, PLS = Polling) that never enumerates because the
driver stops at port 3. The port register blocks are contiguous at a 0x10
stride, so reading one more is safe.

Only raise the limit for a device tree that opts in with
"amlogic,extra-usb-port", and only when the controller really reported fewer
than four ports.

--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
@@ -5478,6 +5478,16 @@
 	xhci->dma_mask_bits = 64;
 	xhci->max_slots = HCS_MAX_SLOTS(hcs_params1);
 	xhci->max_ports = HCS_MAX_PORTS(hcs_params1);
+	/*
+	 * Some X96Max_P boards wire a fourth USB2 port that the controller does
+	 * not advertise: hcs_params1 reports MaxPorts = 3, yet PORTSC4 shows a
+	 * connected device that never enumerates because the driver stops at
+	 * port 3. The port register blocks are contiguous at a 0x10 stride, so
+	 * reading one more is safe; only do it when the platform asks.
+	 */
+	if (xhci->max_ports < 4 && dev->of_node &&
+	    of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node, "amlogic,extra-usb-port"))
+		xhci->max_ports = 4;
 	/* xhci-plat or xhci-pci might have set max_interrupters already */
 	if ((!xhci->max_interrupters) || xhci->max_interrupters > HCS_MAX_INTRS(hcs_params1))
 		xhci->max_interrupters = HCS_MAX_INTRS(hcs_params1);
